WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.876
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.876 --> 00:00:06.758
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you, thank you.

00:00:06.758 --> 00:00:16.758
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:18.715 --> 00:00:20.852
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:20.852 --> 00:00:22.060
Thank you.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:23.058
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you, thank you.

00:00:23.058 --> 00:00:23.755
Thank you Dean Saloner.

00:00:23.755 --> 00:00:24.683
I appreciate that.

00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:25.984
Hi, hi.

00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:29.341
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm so happy to be here.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:31.940
&gt;&gt; Let's jump right in.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:38.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well first, I just met Coach and
he's a cool dude too.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:45.200
And both Coach Dawkins, all my guys,
Coach Shaw, I love those guys.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.515
Clap, you got great coaches here.

00:00:47.515 --> 00:00:54.160
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; That was one of my highlights.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.240
I hope I get to meet him.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.141
I met his mom, but I didn't meet him.

00:00:58.141 --> 00:01:00.017
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry.

00:01:00.017 --> 00:01:04.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I'm giggly like [SOUND].

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.880
&gt;&gt; All right,
well then let's start with sports.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.320
Let's start with basketball.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.490
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.880
&gt;&gt; Many people may not realize that
you actually began preparing for

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.650
your second career, your career in
business, while you were still in the NBA.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.930
Can you tell us what that preparation
looked like and what motivated you?

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:27.240
&gt;&gt; Well, first we have to understand
that the same principles really apply

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.350
from being a basketball
player as well as a CEO.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:38.120
When you think about focus,
strategy, discipline, so

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:43.110
I'm still that same guy that has
those same principles as a CEO.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:49.330
And what I was doing as a basketball
player first and also a competitor.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:52.263
because I hate to lose at anything,
all right?

00:01:52.263 --> 00:01:56.933
Like my wife encouraged me to play my
daughter one on one when she was about 15,

00:01:56.933 --> 00:01:58.190
16 years old.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:04.860
You know, I play her you know,
we go to ten.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.815
I let her get to nine.

00:02:06.815 --> 00:02:08.845
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then I gotta to crush her.

00:02:08.845 --> 00:02:12.451
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And if I was playing my mother,

00:02:12.451 --> 00:02:18.720
I would let her get to nine and
a half, and then I'd crush her too.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.509
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I love winning.

00:02:21.509 --> 00:02:26.652
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I took that same competitive

00:02:26.652 --> 00:02:31.106
spirit to the boardroom as well.

00:02:31.106 --> 00:02:36.578
I'm a perfectionist.

00:02:36.578 --> 00:02:39.159
So I was a perfectionist
as a basketball player.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:44.948
I did the drills over,
and over, and over again.

00:02:44.948 --> 00:02:50.308
And even today, I'm a perfectionist.

00:02:50.308 --> 00:02:53.560
And I want to do everything the right way.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:58.380
So when you think about
principles of being an athlete or

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.050
a basketball player,
it's the same thing as a CEO.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.500
I am a guy who is going
to get there early.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.935
I am not about, I'm a professional.

00:03:10.935 --> 00:03:12.932
Today they said, okay about 11:30.

00:03:12.932 --> 00:03:15.997
Well I got on campus at five to 11.

00:03:15.997 --> 00:03:18.817
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I just sat at the chapel for

00:03:18.817 --> 00:03:20.980
a half hour waiting.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.020
But, that's who I am.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.770
And so I think that I was preparing.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:30.690
What I did was asked Dr. Bus I said,
my dream is not to just win championships,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:35.970
but I want to become a businessman after
my basketball career with the Lakers.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:41.130
And he took me up under his wing and
became my mentor.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.880
One of my many mentors.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.160
And so he taught me Laker business,
opened the books up to me,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.790
let me see how the Laker business
was being run day to day.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:56.370
Then I took, and
I was one of these guys that was crazy so

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:01.760
I called the PR man from the Lakers and
I said look I want to know, all

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.510
the people who sit on the floor, I want to
know their phone numbers and names.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:11.494
So I cold called 20 people, and
said, will you go to lunch with me?

00:04:11.494 --> 00:04:15.011
And they said yeah, so.

00:04:15.011 --> 00:04:19.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Surprise surprise.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.475
&gt;&gt; I took advantage of my platform,
trust me.

00:04:21.475 --> 00:04:24.000
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.200
I picked their brain about business,
what made them successful and

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:33.050
I incorporated it into my own style.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:40.380
So while I was playing basketball,
yes, I was getting ready.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.278
But make no mistake about it,
I was 150% in

00:04:44.278 --> 00:04:48.818
with being a Laker and being the best
basketball player I could be.

00:04:48.818 --> 00:04:54.640
In the off-season, I was preparing for
this life after basketball.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.060
But I was a crazy basketball player,
much like I am CEO.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.825
Because, when you're trying
to prove people wrong.

00:05:02.825 --> 00:05:06.795
First they said I wouldn't be
able to play point guard at 6'9".

00:05:06.795 --> 00:05:08.220
So I had to prove them wrong.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:13.690
Then, they said I couldn't go from
the basketball court to the boardroom.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.880
And it's been my pleasure
proving them wrong again.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:23.700
And so, I'm a guy who goes all the way in.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.020
And I love doing what I'm doing, but

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.130
basketball prepared me for
the business room.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:30.900
No question about it.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.380
If it wasn't for basketball, I don't
think I could be the CEO that I am today.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.842
&gt;&gt; I think you touch on some
good lessons that you learned in

00:05:40.842 --> 00:05:43.387
transitioning your career.

00:05:43.387 --> 00:05:46.587
There's a lot of people who come to
business school hoping to pivot out of one

00:05:46.587 --> 00:05:48.843
career and into another,
basketball pun intended.

00:05:48.843 --> 00:05:52.117
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And what would be-

00:05:52.117 --> 00:05:53.859
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:05:53.859 --> 00:05:58.211
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What would be your top piece of

00:05:58.211 --> 00:05:58.840
advice for them?

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.760
&gt;&gt; Well first of all,
you gotta take your ego out of it.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:09.830
See, what made me a great basketball
player, wouldn't make me a great CEO.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:13.330
I have to understand,
because I didn't know business.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:17.250
So that's why those mentors were so
important to me.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.966
And then, somebody finally
is going to say no to you.

00:06:21.966 --> 00:06:24.012
Mm.

00:06:24.012 --> 00:06:28.104
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:06:28.104 --> 00:06:33.480
then, you have to have a thirst for
knowledge, all right?

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.370
And so, that's why I was meeting with so
many people, because I was hungry for

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:42.870
knowledge, and
I wasn't shy about asking questions.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:48.480
And so I think for me, it was just
making sure I got in the right room with

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:55.690
the right people, make sure I got enough
experts in terms of being my mentors.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:56.750
Experts in business.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:04.140
And then make sure that then I
apply what I learned from them.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:08.840
A lot of times athletes,
we're such in our own world

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:12.700
that we forget that after we retire,

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:19.380
you're still young and
you have a whole life to live.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:28.200
I retired in my 30s and so, I still had
a lot of life and a lot of living to do.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:29.240
And so

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:33.630
I'm so happy that my, I knew that my
second life was going to be in business.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.710
I didn't think it was
going to get to this level.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:40.070
I never thought in my wildest dreams
it would get to where I am today.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:43.241
But I knew I wanted to
be a business man and

00:07:43.241 --> 00:07:48.291
I was going to do everything in my
power to become a successful one.

00:07:48.291 --> 00:07:50.984
&gt;&gt; Well you did become very successful.

00:07:50.984 --> 00:07:53.910
And I want to play back to
earlier in your career.

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:57.839
You had an early partnership
with Starbucks, and around 1998,

00:07:57.839 --> 00:08:01.560
you opened 125 stores for
them in low income neighborhoods.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.520
It was the first time Starbucks
had done something like that.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:08.200
Your stores were more successful
than the national average.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:09.530
You don't own those anymore, but

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:13.650
can you talk about how important
that deal was in your career?

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:18.401
&gt;&gt; Well, let me go back before
that one because actually

00:08:18.401 --> 00:08:22.210
the Magic Johnson Theatre set up that one.

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:25.090
And I did my homework and research and

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:28.080
found out minorities were the number one
group of people going to the movies at

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.849
that time, but we didn't have no
theaters built in our community.

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:35.540
And I said wow, if we're the number
one people going to the theater,

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:39.510
to the movies, if I build them,
they will come.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.270
The demand was already there.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.680
So I met with the Sony execs and
told them,

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:49.100
look, the growth of your business
has to be through urban America.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:50.680
We've got an unbelievable spending power.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.760
Look at our spending power right now.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:55.430
African Americans, a trillion
dollars spending power, Latinos,

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:57.740
another trillion dollars spending power,
and growing.

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:03.350
And I'm sitting there saying, wow,
that's a lot of disposable income,

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:09.630
and that could make my business
become successful very quick.

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.340
And so they said let's do it.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:13.330
I put up half the money.

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:15.330
They put up half the money.

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:17.240
And the first thing people said,
oh, no way.

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:18.960
They're going to tear it up.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:22.850
Graffiti going to be all on the walls.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:27.600
And what happened was my first theater
in Crenshaw in LA was in the top

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.689
ten highest grossing
theaters in the nation.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:36.670
And what we had to do was tweak
the concession stand a little bit.

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:38.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; because minorities,

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.395
we're a little bit different
than everybody else, so.

00:09:40.395 --> 00:09:43.905
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We grew up on Kool-Aid, so

00:09:43.905 --> 00:09:48.960
I had to have strawberry,
grape, orange, soda.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:50.600
We don't drink just coke.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:51.298
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:09:51.298 --> 00:09:52.281
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So.

00:09:52.281 --> 00:09:56.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now,

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.670
the reason I said that because my per caps
were some of the highest in the industry.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:10.440
Wait a minute, so if I keep that coke
just in there, and my customer base

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:16.160
don't drink coke,
I'm not successful, right?

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:19.210
Then I had a spicier hot dog.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:22.980
Some jalapeno peppers,

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:27.790
that was the first time in theater
history that ever happened, right?

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:33.760
And so I was able to understand my
customer base and overdeliver to them.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:37.790
And I'm going to use that word all
the time, and you students out there,

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:41.600
I want you to write that word down,
and that's overdeliver.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.630
because if you going to be in business,
and

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:51.649
especially today's marketplace,
you gotta overdeliver to the consumer.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:55.110
Or guess what,
they're going to leave you that fast.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:11:02.540
I have overdelivered to my
customer base for 30 years.

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:04.840
That's why I have the number
one brand in urban America.

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:09.840
And so,
if I had have just kept coke in there,

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:13.620
not the hot dog change,
it would have been a problem.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:16.350
Let me tell you a little quick story about

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.770
how they looked at me
as a basketball player.

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:21.548
So we're about to open my first
theater on Crenshaw in LA.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.840
And I asked the food buyer from Sony,
I said, how many hot dogs do we have for

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:27.510
the opening?

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:31.140
So he's like, man, the same amount

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:35.890
we are going to give you is the same
amount we have in our suburban theaters.

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:40.850
We opened the theater on Friday,
we sold out all the hot dogs

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:45.840
that normally take a month to sell out
in suburban, sold out in one night.

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:48.250
Why?
Because minorities,

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:49.705
we're not going to go to dinner and
a movie.

00:11:49.705 --> 00:11:55.960
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We're going to have dinner where?

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:58.391
&gt;&gt; At the movies.
&gt;&gt; I can't hear you.

00:11:58.391 --> 00:12:00.045
&gt;&gt; At the movies.

00:12:00.045 --> 00:12:04.680
So that's another reason
my per caps were so high.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:10.010
You have to have an understanding
of your customer base.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:15.640
And I know what urban consumers want,
and I deliver that.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:20.970
So because we were very successful
with the Magic Johnson Theatres,

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:23.710
I went up to Seattle
because I saw this long

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:26.750
line outside this coffee
place called Starbucks.

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:32.755
I'm like, man,
what kind of coffee they serving in there?

00:12:32.755 --> 00:12:34.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:39.170
I said, I gotta get in that line,
I gotta see what's going on.

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:43.920
So I got in the line and
waiting and waiting and waiting.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:47.090
I finally got up there, and
I don't drink coffee, I drink tea.

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:49.780
So I said, man,
let me have your best tea, whatever.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:52.626
I took a sip of that tea.

00:12:52.626 --> 00:12:53.195
I said, wow.

00:12:53.195 --> 00:12:55.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No wonder.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:13:00.960
I said, man, that's the next successful
thing that can happen in urban America.

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:05.900
So I cold called Howard Schultz,
CEO of Starbucks and

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:09.100
said look, I want to come up and see you.

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:12.450
So I flew up to Seattle, and
we had a meeting, and thank god,

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:18.600
he was a basketball fan and a season
ticket holder of Seattle SuperSonics.

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:20.060
And we sat down and talked.

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:22.725
And I told him the growth of this
business would be through urban America.

00:13:22.725 --> 00:13:29.220
because Starbucks got a store, man,
they saturate the market, which is great.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:30.660
They dominate the market.

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:36.190
I said, but there's none in urban America,
and we got this great spending power,

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:38.480
and I knew that it would be
successful in urban America.

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:41.470
He said, well, we don't do franchisees.

00:13:41.470 --> 00:13:42.840
I said, I'm not here to become one.

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:43.780
I want to be your partner.

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:45.530
I'll put up half the money,
you put up half the money.

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:48.868
So, he said, well,
I've got to sell it to the board,

00:13:48.868 --> 00:13:50.392
let's have a couple more meetings.

00:13:50.392 --> 00:13:53.452
So long story short,
we had about four more meetings, and

00:13:53.452 --> 00:13:57.790
then he finally decided to come down and
see how we managed my theaters.

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:00.690
And this is really important to
a lot of you young people out there.

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:04.730
You've got to make sure
that what you selling and

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:07.970
what you tell people is
going to be the truth, right?

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:12.700
When they come and see what your business
looks like, everything that you told them,

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:14.390
it sure better look like that.

00:14:14.390 --> 00:14:17.060
So sure enough,
he comes down on a Friday night, and

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:21.065
thank god, Whitney Houston's first movie
was coming out, Waiting to Exhale.

00:14:21.065 --> 00:14:22.856
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I had about 4 or

00:14:22.856 --> 00:14:27.581
5,000 African American women wrapped
around the corner trying to get in.

00:14:27.581 --> 00:14:31.122
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; To see this movie.

00:14:31.122 --> 00:14:34.607
So Howard comes,
we come around the corner.

00:14:34.607 --> 00:14:38.237
He's like wow, man.

00:14:38.237 --> 00:14:42.200
He was amazed we had this many
people waiting to see this movie.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:45.830
Got inside, the concession stand was just,

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:52.280
I mean we were making money like crazy
because everybody was ordering something.

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:52.910
And then finally,

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:57.900
the movie started, so we sat in one of
my biggest house was like 500 seats.

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:03.650
And the movie got going,
about 20 minutes in.

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:06.650
And every African American
woman in there thought they

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:10.835
knew Whitney Houston personally, so
they start talking to the screen.

00:15:10.835 --> 00:15:13.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Girl, why you still with him?

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:17.750
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You should dump him.

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:20.840
And so Howard grabs me out the theater.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:21.398
True story.

00:15:21.398 --> 00:15:25.390
And said, Irving,

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:30.240
I've never had a movie going
experience quite like this.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:31.650
And he said, you got the deal.

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:35.810
So we did three as a test.

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:40.150
And this is where that word
comes in young people.

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:41.990
So he gave me three to test.

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:46.841
And I had to overdeliver with the three.

00:15:46.841 --> 00:15:49.898
So when I looked up, I said okay,

00:15:49.898 --> 00:15:56.018
we gotta tweak Starbucks just
a little bit to fit In urban America,

00:15:56.018 --> 00:16:00.410
the coffee was great,
but the dessert was not.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:04.205
We don't quite know what scones are.

00:16:04.205 --> 00:16:07.154
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I had to take the scone's out of my

00:16:07.154 --> 00:16:14.320
Starbucks and put sweet potato pie, pound
cakes, sock it to me cake, peach cobbler.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:18.930
They said no way minorities will
pay $3 for a cup of coffee.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:22.540
Well, we proved everybody wrong.

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:25.789
My per caps were higher than his per caps.

00:16:25.789 --> 00:16:31.844
And, it went from building
3 to building 125.

00:16:31.844 --> 00:16:34.816
The multiple was already negotiated.

00:16:34.816 --> 00:16:38.039
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So when the [LAUGH]-

00:16:38.039 --> 00:16:42.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:44.877
&gt;&gt; So when my contract was up in terms of

00:16:44.877 --> 00:16:48.860
the number, 125,
I sold them all back to Howard.

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:54.215
And I walked away,
with a big smile on my face.

00:16:54.215 --> 00:16:57.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And he did too.

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:03.740
Because he says already,
in all the interviews that he's been able

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:09.030
to do after our partnership, he said how
our stores really changed Starbucks.

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:16.072
Brought in a lot of minority managers,
district managers, employees.

00:17:16.072 --> 00:17:20.680
And now he's built now,
500 to a 1000 stores in urban America.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:22.200
And it's great.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:27.280
So its great for
me to be the guy who opened the door for

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:29.660
Starbucks going into urban America.

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:31.895
And we should clap for
that because that's that's a great thing.

00:17:31.895 --> 00:17:37.307
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:17:37.307 --> 00:17:40.000
&gt;&gt; So, it's still about overdelivering.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:45.557
See, if I had of kept
those scones in there,

00:17:45.557 --> 00:17:50.674
my per caps wouldn't have been 459.

00:17:50.674 --> 00:17:51.710
It wouldn't have been that high.

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:57.516
So, because I changed
out those desserts and

00:17:57.516 --> 00:18:02.946
then I also took out the Lawrence Welk
music that they played in-

00:18:02.946 --> 00:18:04.133
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:18:04.133 --> 00:18:06.263
&gt;&gt; The regular Starbucks and

00:18:06.263 --> 00:18:10.421
I put in Prince, Motown music,
Earth, Wind, and

00:18:10.421 --> 00:18:15.650
Fire, things that resonate
with the urban consumer.

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:18.650
So now if I got you sitting there, and

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:22.270
you made your first purchase,
and you're comfortable.

00:18:22.270 --> 00:18:27.616
And you got your head moving, and you've
got your favorite drink next to you,

00:18:27.616 --> 00:18:32.166
you're going to sit there long
enough to make your second purchase.

00:18:32.166 --> 00:18:36.870
And then minorities,
if we call a place, that's my place.

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:40.200
Like, that's my restaurant,
that's my burger place.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:45.083
Whatever it is, that's where
we're going to go all the time

00:18:45.083 --> 00:18:48.480
because they've overdelivered to us.

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:53.299
And you're the same way.

00:18:53.299 --> 00:18:56.254
And so,
that's why you have to overdeliver.

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:00.150
And also,
stay in touch with that customer base.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:04.370
In terms of asking them questions,
do you like this?

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:05.900
Do you like that?

00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:11.210
We always stay intune and in touch
with our customer base to make sure

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:14.910
we're doing the right thing, and
that's why I've been successful, as well.

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:19.430
&gt;&gt; I hear you talking about
diversity of perspective and

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:21.520
how important that was for you.

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:26.492
There's been a focus recently on the lack
of diversity within Silicon Valley and

00:19:26.492 --> 00:19:28.405
the tech industry in general.

00:19:28.405 --> 00:19:32.512
How does that play into your
investment and partnership decisions,

00:19:32.512 --> 00:19:35.201
and also how do you think
about recruiting and

00:19:35.201 --> 00:19:39.473
retaining diverse talent now that
your head of your own organization?

00:19:39.473 --> 00:19:43.650
&gt;&gt; Well the first one is,
when you think about Silicon Valley and

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:48.792
you think about what is only 1% of
engineers that are African Americans and

00:19:48.792 --> 00:19:50.910
about 3% I think is Latinos.

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:52.460
Now that's way too low.

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:54.635
But they keep using that as an excuse.

00:19:54.635 --> 00:19:58.133
Well we can't find enough
engineers who are minorities.

00:19:58.133 --> 00:19:58.940
Okay, that's fine.

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:08.090
But what about, you have lawyers,
accountants, the HR department,

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:13.200
there's other areas that you can hire
minorities who are qualified, right?

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:16.700
And allow them sit on this campus too.

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:19.650
And other campuses.

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:26.063
So, we just don't want to be engineers.

00:20:26.063 --> 00:20:27.260
We'd love to work at Google.

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:31.950
We would love to work at Facebook and
on and on and on.

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:35.290
And so, diversity starts at the top.

00:20:35.290 --> 00:20:40.400
If it's not a part of your DNA, it's not
who you are, it's not going to happen.

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:43.180
That's why I love Stanford, see?

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:45.760
And we don't want a hand out.

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:47.300
We just want an opportunity.

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:48.490
Now look at those two coaches.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:53.435
They just wanted the opportunity, and
they're delivering, overdelivering.

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:55.420
And that's all.

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:59.330
We just ask for an opportunity.

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:02.699
Look, you want to hire
the way America looks.

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:08.990
In the next twenty years,
half of America or

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:12.180
even more than half of America,
is going to be minorities.

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:18.670
So, the first companies
that understand that, and

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:23.730
start hiring minorities now, they will
understand how to really deal with them

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:26.140
not only as employees but
also as consumers.

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:32.985
And so for me, it's easy for me.

00:21:32.985 --> 00:21:34.654
I like to hire smart, young people.

00:21:34.654 --> 00:21:38.105
Matter of fact, I got one of your
own that work for me, stand on up.

00:21:38.105 --> 00:21:41.456
Yeah, Ryan, yeah [COUGH].

00:21:41.456 --> 00:21:46.975
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And

00:21:46.975 --> 00:21:50.690
Ryan, I'll tell you
what's great about Ryan.

00:21:50.690 --> 00:21:54.940
Ryan was here on the campus
a couple years ago,

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:59.390
and he was doing some internship work for
us, right?

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:03.750
And so, that's when we were
thinking about buying the Sparks.

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:10.143
So, we put Ryan on the Sparks deal.

00:22:10.143 --> 00:22:13.509
He analyzed the numbers,
he came back to myself and

00:22:13.509 --> 00:22:18.206
said, he thought that the number that
we were going to pay for the Sparks,

00:22:18.206 --> 00:22:20.650
it was going to be a good deal.

00:22:20.650 --> 00:22:24.920
If he had said, don't do the Sparks deal,
I wouldn't have done the Sparks deal.

00:22:24.920 --> 00:22:28.680
So, we got young,
bright minds like Ryan in our company.

00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:34.667
And so Stanford you're
producing some great, young,

00:22:34.667 --> 00:22:39.820
smart, also too he's single so
any ladies out there [LAUGH].

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:44.149
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] They

00:22:44.149 --> 00:22:48.881
start clapping.

00:22:48.881 --> 00:22:54.470
And then once he analyzed that deal and
broke it down for me.

00:22:54.470 --> 00:23:00.040
I knew that I wanted him because you have
to have people skills to work for me too.

00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:01.380
It not just being smart.

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:06.620
I want somebody who can also go out and
on behalf of our company, represent us.

00:23:06.620 --> 00:23:09.050
And he does a wonderful job of that.

00:23:09.050 --> 00:23:13.625
And so,
my brand is everything to me, and so

00:23:13.625 --> 00:23:19.827
I gotta make sure that the thing
that I protect is the brand.

00:23:19.827 --> 00:23:21.906
See, I have everything else.

00:23:21.906 --> 00:23:23.700
Sustainability, I have growth.

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:26.190
I have all that now, right?

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:34.360
But, the one thing that could ruin it all,
is a tainted brand and a diluted brand.

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:38.127
And so, I want to make sure
that I protect that brand and

00:23:38.127 --> 00:23:43.152
that's why I hire people like Ryan who
can not only protect the brand but

00:23:43.152 --> 00:23:47.209
grow the brand, and
the bottom line all at the same time.

00:23:47.209 --> 00:23:52.457
And so, we're buying the Dodgers,
that's sustainability.

00:23:52.457 --> 00:23:57.446
I have a $14 billion Insurance
company that I just bought

00:23:57.446 --> 00:24:00.630
called Equitrust out of Iowa.

00:24:00.630 --> 00:24:01.700
That's our headquarters.

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:03.720
We're going to move that to Illinois.

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:06.630
It's doing gang buster business.

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:08.700
We're growing it.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:14.400
So, when I look at it I have
everything that I want now.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:20.080
I have I was fighting for first respect
because they didn't respect me when I

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:24.390
first came into the boardroom because they
still look at me as a basketball player.

00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:30.170
But that Starbucks deal changed
all of that, see I drove ROI.

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:37.540
I took Howard's brand, it was already huge
but I took that brand to another level.

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:39.400
It was the first company ever.

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:43.022
Jay Leno picked up a Starbucks cup,
live on TV, and

00:24:43.022 --> 00:24:48.761
said why you charging those people that
kind of money for a cup of coffee, right.

00:24:48.761 --> 00:24:52.988
And we all laughed but
Howard Shaw was happy, like, wow.

00:24:52.988 --> 00:24:56.587
I was just on the Tonight Show and
didn't cost me a dollar.

00:24:56.587 --> 00:25:00.040
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You see what I'm saying?

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:04.790
So we were able to grow
the brand of Starbucks for

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:09.070
free, and also at the end of

00:25:09.070 --> 00:25:13.970
the day also be able to grow the bottom
line too, and that's why that

00:25:13.970 --> 00:25:19.310
partnership was the one partnership that
really got me everything that I wanted.

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:22.740
And then,
I've had the number one real estate

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:29.370
when you think about fund
in terms of urban fund.

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:33.510
So, I did, we did 300,000,000,
we did 600,000,000 our second fund.

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:35.510
And our third fund,
we did a billion dollar.

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:40.270
And then, we had an equity fund worth
around Burkle's company, Yucaipa Johnson.

00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:43.100
So, we went out and
raised about 500 million.

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:49.530
So that's when I bought a lot of the,
we bought about 20 radio stations,

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:51.820
we bought Soul Train brand on and
on and on.

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:57.460
With the real estate fund,
if you think about the W in Austin Texas,

00:25:57.460 --> 00:26:00.230
you think about the Hilton
in Washington D.C.,

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:03.570
all those things,
all those properties we still own.

00:26:03.570 --> 00:26:08.490
And the Trans America Building downtown
Los Angeles, we used to own that.

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:12.140
And so, those are the type of
properties that we invested in.

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:16.640
And so, it was really unique and

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:21.760
special that I can go from the basketball
court dribbling that little basketball to

00:26:21.760 --> 00:26:28.140
having a real estate fund, having an
equity fund, and now I'm with Guggenheim.

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:33.010
And Mark Walter told me, you don't have
to go out and raise money no more.

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:36.874
We manage about $200 billion,
just come see us, okay.

00:26:36.874 --> 00:26:42.060
[LAUGH] And I tell you,
this is a quick story for some of you.

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:44.090
because some of you
going to be disappointed.

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:45.215
I gotta stand up for a minute.

00:26:45.215 --> 00:26:47.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Is that okay?

00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:48.272
&gt;&gt; Yes.
[LAUGH]

00:26:48.272 --> 00:26:49.904
&gt;&gt; I know I'm at Stanford and

00:26:49.904 --> 00:26:52.490
everything so all these smart people.

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:54.250
because I might be working for
one of them one day.

00:26:55.920 --> 00:26:58.180
You don't mind me changing
a little bit Dean do you?

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:01.368
Okay cool, because I can't
just sit down all the time.

00:27:01.368 --> 00:27:04.644
Young people.

00:27:04.644 --> 00:27:07.490
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:09.680
when I first went out
to raise my first fund

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:17.160
I went up to Cal Pers,
right down the road from here.

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:23.310
And they turned me down, four times.

00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:28.020
And the last time
the committee turned me down

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:32.250
they said well why haven't
some other minority come

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:38.150
to raise money and
do what you're talking about doing?

00:27:39.650 --> 00:27:44.200
And I couldn't answer that
question I was up there to

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:50.990
have them give me around $50,000,000
to invest in Urban America.

00:27:50.990 --> 00:27:52.510
That was unheard of at that time.

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:58.540
And so, finally I went up,
the time after that, the fifth time and

00:27:58.540 --> 00:28:02.220
they finally said, you know what,
we're going to give you the 50 million.

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:08.230
And if you, I'm going to come back
to that word, if you over deliver

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:11.810
with the 50 million, you can come back and
get 100 more million.

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:15.700
I bought a shopping center for 22 million,

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:20.390
it was 40% occupied.

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:23.560
I made that center 100% occupied.

00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:26.420
I resold that center for $48 million.

00:28:26.420 --> 00:28:30.745
Took the 26 back up to Sacramento and
said, here you go.

00:28:30.745 --> 00:28:33.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And guess what happened.

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:37.560
They started respecting
me like a businessman.

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:40.900
Now, a lot of you going to get
turned down five times like I did.

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:44.086
Are you going to get back up and
try again?

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:50.852
Or you going to go,
have your head down, disappointed, and

00:28:50.852 --> 00:28:52.830
not get up and try again?

00:28:54.910 --> 00:28:59.090
See, I knew I had a great business plan,
I knew I had a great strategy.

00:28:59.090 --> 00:28:59.959
And I believed in it.

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:04.270
That's why I kept going up to Sacramento.

00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:09.180
And finally they said yes and
that's when I took off.

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:13.990
So remember, you going to get turned down.

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.190
Somebody not going to
like your business plan.

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:19.080
That's all right.

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:21.920
You can tweak it,
you can make adjustments to that.

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:24.920
But keep going if you believe in it.

00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:29.310
And if you've done your homework and
your research and you find that

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:34.060
you have a good chance to be successful
with your business, keep going.

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:39.060
Never let anybody define who
you can become and who you are.

00:29:40.870 --> 00:29:42.436
They said oh Magic just a point guard.

00:29:42.436 --> 00:29:43.829
No.

00:29:43.829 --> 00:29:47.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was more than that.

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:53.720
And now I'm proving it each and every day.

00:29:55.050 --> 00:29:58.420
Jack Dorsey just said, hey, Magic,
come on, join my board of square.

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:02.170
I've been turning down boards left and
right, and

00:30:02.170 --> 00:30:08.740
I finally believed in in him and
what he's built and so I finally said yes.

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:15.194
But if I hadn't proven myself,
he would never come and

00:30:15.194 --> 00:30:20.360
ask me to join his board of square, okay.

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:21.135
Back to you now.

00:30:21.135 --> 00:30:22.620
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:30:22.620 --> 00:30:24.340
&gt;&gt; See that's how we do it at Stanford.

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:26.896
We-
&gt;&gt; Experience through learning.

00:30:26.896 --> 00:30:33.430
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:30:33.430 --> 00:30:34.980
everything-
&gt;&gt; Something I live by too,

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:36.340
young people write this down.

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:39.391
Always be ready to adapt
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:30:39.391 --> 00:30:40.831
&gt;&gt; And adjust.

00:30:40.831 --> 00:30:44.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm serious.

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:48.130
Market change,
you got to be ready to adapt and adjust.

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:50.860
And we did that.

00:30:50.860 --> 00:30:54.750
With my real estate fund,
we had built some condos.

00:30:57.370 --> 00:30:59.330
The market just, remember,

00:30:59.330 --> 00:31:04.330
banks weren't loaning, no more money,
and everybody was in trouble.

00:31:05.870 --> 00:31:07.444
But quickly we said oh,

00:31:07.444 --> 00:31:12.030
wait a minute we can change to
a renter's model [SOUND] Just that fast.

00:31:12.030 --> 00:31:13.930
And guess what happened?

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:16.750
It didn't affect our bottom line.

00:31:16.750 --> 00:31:20.924
We still were successful because
we were smart enough to say okay,

00:31:20.924 --> 00:31:23.980
it's not going to work
because nobody can get loans.

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:28.749
But we can go to a renter's model and
it can still work for us.

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:35.930
Make sure you're quick and
nimble enough to say, you know what?

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:40.000
I can adapt and adjust to what's
going on in the market place today.

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:45.936
I can change my business, or
tweak my business to make it still work.

00:31:45.936 --> 00:31:49.931
&gt;&gt; You're talking about
resiliency throughout that and

00:31:49.931 --> 00:31:55.061
I want to shift to a more, Personal
issue before we turn to questions.

00:31:55.061 --> 00:31:57.270
And that is your health.

00:31:57.270 --> 00:32:03.180
And you contracted HIV at the height
of your super-stardom in basketball and

00:32:03.180 --> 00:32:08.420
overnight you became the face of a disease
that at the time people thought was a gay,

00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:10.330
white man's disease.

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:13.860
Can you talk about the experience
of having to become a leader

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:17.500
in a community that you might have
otherwise never been associated with?

00:32:17.500 --> 00:32:18.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:19.180
Well, first it was tough.

00:32:19.180 --> 00:32:22.820
It was tough because in the beginning,

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:29.030
getting that phone call,
come to the doctors office,

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:33.350
we're in Utah, fly all the way home
from Utah, couldn't play in the game.

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:36.680
I didn't know what was wrong,
what was going on.

00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:40.570
And to walk into the doctor's office and
him to tell me I have HIV,

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:43.640
it was just devastating.

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:46.590
I mean, I'm talking about I
was on the floor devastated.

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:53.710
And it took me hours just to come to
grips with what he had just said.

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:58.900
And then what was more devastating than
that was, I had just gotten married,

00:32:58.900 --> 00:32:59.700
my wife was pregnant.

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:02.710
So now I've gotta drive home
to tell her I have HIV.

00:33:04.670 --> 00:33:08.520
And more than thinking about myself,

00:33:08.520 --> 00:33:12.230
I was thinking about her and
my baby at that time.

00:33:14.050 --> 00:33:19.700
And I tell you, I never thought and

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:25.560
I had did everything right, I thought.

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:29.550
I was married to the game,
I put in maximum effort.

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:34.890
And I'm now, boom.

00:33:34.890 --> 00:33:35.940
This happens.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:39.340
I married the woman that I
met on Michigan State campus.

00:33:40.890 --> 00:33:43.830
That I've always wanted
to marry my best friend.

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:49.680
All those things are going through
your mind as you're driving home

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:54.820
to now face the toughest
challenge in my life.

00:33:54.820 --> 00:33:56.828
More than Michael, more than Larry.

00:33:56.828 --> 00:34:02.933
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:02.933 --> 00:34:07.040
[LAUGH] Yeah, I'm glad I can
laugh about it now, I tell you.

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:13.990
But it was, when I hit the door,
she knew something was wrong.

00:34:13.990 --> 00:34:14.569
She knew it.

00:34:15.740 --> 00:34:22.070
And so I sat her down, and I began to tell
her I had HIV and she cried like a baby.

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:24.570
And the thing that makes it tough,
young people,

00:34:26.640 --> 00:34:30.670
your mistake not only hurts you,
but those who love you.

00:34:30.670 --> 00:34:32.220
It hurts them too.

00:34:32.220 --> 00:34:34.989
They are affected by your mistakes.

00:34:36.850 --> 00:34:42.740
And here she is, been nothing but
a great wife, great friend and

00:34:42.740 --> 00:34:48.209
I'm putting her at risk and
putting her in this situation.

00:34:50.345 --> 00:34:53.793
As we're talking about it and
I'm trying to console her, and

00:34:53.793 --> 00:34:56.659
then I told her I understand
if you want to leave me.

00:34:59.070 --> 00:35:02.270
And then she hit me so
hard up side my head and she said,

00:35:02.270 --> 00:35:04.343
we're going to beat this together.

00:35:04.343 --> 00:35:10.130
This is the only time I knew that I
was going to have a chance to live.

00:35:11.820 --> 00:35:16.710
Because if Cookie had have left,
I don't think I would be here.

00:35:17.960 --> 00:35:23.450
Because I needed her as a support system.

00:35:23.450 --> 00:35:27.500
And that was a difficult time, so

00:35:27.500 --> 00:35:33.940
you need the ones that love you to help
you through your toughest moments.

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:39.450
And so I'm so happy she stayed and
supported me.

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:43.420
And then, we got the best news
about a couple weeks later.

00:35:43.420 --> 00:35:49.720
She was okay, the baby was okay,
after the doctors ran the test.

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:55.530
So I've been the face of this
disease now for 24 years.

00:35:55.530 --> 00:35:59.590
&gt;&gt; Was she also the person who
encourage you to speak out and

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:01.510
help to educate everyone about AIDS?

00:36:01.510 --> 00:36:02.960
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:04.210
Great question!

00:36:04.210 --> 00:36:09.160
We went back and forth,
once we found out her status,

00:36:09.160 --> 00:36:12.960
we went back and
forth on if we should go public or not.

00:36:12.960 --> 00:36:16.270
So I met with a lady who was
living with AIDS at that time,

00:36:16.270 --> 00:36:18.330
Elizabeth Glazier, changed my whole life.

00:36:19.330 --> 00:36:22.540
She was dying at that time and

00:36:22.540 --> 00:36:27.570
she told me Earvin,
I want you to be the face of the disease,

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:31.249
they need a face, it needs a face and
you will be perfect.

00:36:32.250 --> 00:36:33.280
You got to go public.

00:36:34.490 --> 00:36:35.800
It's going to help the world.

00:36:37.020 --> 00:36:42.450
And she said there's some great
drugs coming down the pipeline.

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:44.360
And she was right.

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:47.030
At that time there was only
one drug at that time, AZT.

00:36:47.030 --> 00:36:54.290
And so I'm glad she was there for
me as well.

00:36:54.290 --> 00:36:57.890
And then I promised her that I would
become the face, and I would do a lot of

00:36:57.890 --> 00:37:02.090
different things to help those who are
living with it and also to educate people.

00:37:03.410 --> 00:37:08.070
And she has a great foundation named
after her, doing great work too.

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:16.010
And so Cookie and I after meeting with
her, decided to go public at that time.

00:37:16.010 --> 00:37:21.050
And now you look at the numbers
there's over a million people

00:37:21.050 --> 00:37:26.800
living with HIV in this country, but one
out of eight don't even know they have it.

00:37:26.800 --> 00:37:31.230
It's a lot of people walking around
right now they don't know they have HIV.

00:37:31.230 --> 00:37:35.160
And then it's effected the black and
brown community in a big way.

00:37:36.940 --> 00:37:41.940
When you think about the numbers,
especially the numbers of blacks

00:37:41.940 --> 00:37:46.690
in terms of 55% of men,
45% of black women.

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:50.250
So it's really important that
we still go out and get tested.

00:37:51.380 --> 00:37:53.250
Also go back for your results.

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:56.830
Now you can get it
tested in your own home.

00:37:56.830 --> 00:37:58.720
Now instead of one drug,
there's thirty drugs.

00:37:58.720 --> 00:37:59.880
That's why I'm living today.

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:05.230
If you think about the drugs are so
much better today.

00:38:05.230 --> 00:38:06.410
Easier to take today.

00:38:07.650 --> 00:38:14.680
There's still a fear of minorities going
to find out if they have HIV or not.

00:38:14.680 --> 00:38:17.930
That's been really hurting
our community in a big way.

00:38:19.140 --> 00:38:25.130
And so I fight against discrimination
against those who are living with HIV.

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:29.130
I go out, educate people,
and that's what I do.

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:30.169
This is my life.

00:38:31.260 --> 00:38:32.950
And so I love what I do.

00:38:32.950 --> 00:38:38.130
I love going out, talking about HIV and
AIDS and also helping people.

00:38:39.580 --> 00:38:42.260
And that's what I'm all about.

00:38:42.260 --> 00:38:48.070
And then, when I joined
President Bush AIDS Commission,

00:38:48.070 --> 00:38:53.510
back at that time, this is the father,
I remember I went to Boston and

00:38:53.510 --> 00:38:57.490
they had a hospice, 30 beds.

00:38:57.490 --> 00:38:58.420
I walked in.

00:38:58.420 --> 00:39:06.780
Only one bed was,
one patient was in one of the bedrooms.

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:10.129
And I was like,
what's wrong with the other 29 rooms?

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:15.100
They had to take you through a process for
you to get a room.

00:39:16.510 --> 00:39:18.350
I mean don't these people
who need a room and

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:23.330
they had to go through
a process to get one.

00:39:23.330 --> 00:39:28.000
It takes like a year or two to get
cleared, and just that day I quit.

00:39:29.090 --> 00:39:30.660
I said this is not right.

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:35.218
There's people who need rooms who
have to be on the street, and

00:39:35.218 --> 00:39:38.143
we take them through this long process.

00:39:38.143 --> 00:39:42.854
And so I quit that day and
I built a Magic Johnson Foundation, and

00:39:42.854 --> 00:39:48.265
we've already given out over,
almost about $15 million to different

00:39:48.265 --> 00:39:53.170
aids organization We've tested
over 40,000 minorities.

00:39:55.220 --> 00:39:58.360
This is what I do everyday, I love it.

00:39:58.360 --> 00:40:00.370
Not just the HIV and AIDS side,

00:40:00.370 --> 00:40:05.700
we have 120 students on scholarship right
now, colleges all over the country.

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:08.950
We've built 16 technology centers

00:40:10.860 --> 00:40:14.450
because a lot of minorities can't afford
to have a computer in their home.

00:40:14.450 --> 00:40:20.015
We give them access to one and these are
the things that we do every single day.

00:40:20.015 --> 00:40:24.300
Then the last thing I just started was
Magic Johnson Bridgescape Academies

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:28.100
because right now,
the problem we have in urban America is so

00:40:28.100 --> 00:40:30.090
many dropouts right now.

00:40:30.090 --> 00:40:33.790
So we're building these drop back in
centers where kids can get off the street,

00:40:33.790 --> 00:40:37.420
get there high school diploma and it's so

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:40.650
great that we've graduated 400 students so
far.

00:40:40.650 --> 00:40:43.240
And guess what,
a lot of them are in college right now.

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:46.603
We should clap for that.

00:40:46.603 --> 00:40:54.845
[APPLAUSE] [APPLAUSE] We
should go to questions.

00:40:54.845 --> 00:40:58.800
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We don't have much time left but

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:02.720
if you just want to raise your hand, the
people with the mics will come down and

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:05.055
find you and
we'll start with a question from Twitter.

00:41:05.055 --> 00:41:06.570
&gt;&gt; Let's take five or so.

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:09.670
We can't just run off,
I came all the way here from LA.

00:41:09.670 --> 00:41:10.545
That's a long way.

00:41:10.545 --> 00:41:14.550
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And not answer some questions now.

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:20.414
My Dodgers are in the play offs, don't
be booing up here but it's Giant's fans.

00:41:20.414 --> 00:41:22.730
[APPLAUSE] Go ahead.

00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:24.620
&gt;&gt; First question from Twitter.

00:41:24.620 --> 00:41:27.960
What was the hardest lesson you
learned when you transitioned

00:41:27.960 --> 00:41:31.140
from basketball to business?

00:41:31.140 --> 00:41:36.220
&gt;&gt; I thought it would be easier
because I was Magic Johnson but

00:41:36.220 --> 00:41:39.105
actually it was harder.

00:41:39.105 --> 00:41:40.550
Ten banks turned me down.

00:41:40.550 --> 00:41:42.790
Besides, CalPERS turned me down.

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:49.960
I was looking to grow and
I used up just about a lot of my income to

00:41:49.960 --> 00:41:54.810
get me to a level but now,
I wanted to go to another level and

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:58.630
I wanted that growth and
I needed some big money.

00:42:01.640 --> 00:42:06.540
I went to the banks and
ten of them turned me down and

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:11.450
then I said, if ten turn me down, I'm
going to make sure everybody turn me down,

00:42:11.450 --> 00:42:13.560
so I went to the next bank and
they said yes.

00:42:16.910 --> 00:42:17.790
It's really funny.

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:18.345
What's your name?

00:42:18.345 --> 00:42:20.638
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Gracie, it's really funny.

00:42:20.638 --> 00:42:24.413
Now, Gracie,

00:42:24.413 --> 00:42:30.790
after the eleventh bank said yes,
and owning the Dodgers,

00:42:30.790 --> 00:42:35.350
all this stuff, all them ten banks
are knocking on my door saying, oh,

00:42:35.350 --> 00:42:41.270
we'll give you as much lines of
credit as you want, on and on and on.

00:42:41.270 --> 00:42:45.323
But I'm a loyal guy, so
I stayed with who got me here.

00:42:45.323 --> 00:42:50.560
Again, you're going to get
turned down sometimes but

00:42:50.560 --> 00:42:52.510
keep on going because
somebody may say yes.

00:42:53.580 --> 00:42:58.190
I was blessed that somebody said yes and
believed in me and my strategy and

00:42:58.190 --> 00:42:59.550
believed in urban America too.

00:42:59.550 --> 00:43:01.210
So, thank you.

00:43:01.210 --> 00:43:02.530
That, that was the hardest thing.

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:05.920
Who got the next one?

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:06.730
Oh, right here.

00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:07.980
&gt;&gt; Hey.
My name is George.

00:43:07.980 --> 00:43:09.320
I have a question.

00:43:09.320 --> 00:43:12.185
With the Equilife deal, you're-
&gt;&gt; EquiTrust.

00:43:12.185 --> 00:43:13.220
&gt;&gt; EquiTrust deal,

00:43:13.220 --> 00:43:17.100
your organization was the largest
black-owned insurance company in America.

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:20.970
How do you see minority-owned financial
institutions changing the way American

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:21.680
business is done?

00:43:22.770 --> 00:43:26.910
&gt;&gt; I think, first of all,
we gotta be successful, that's number one.

00:43:26.910 --> 00:43:31.592
Always remember that you can only
change things when you're succesful.

00:43:31.592 --> 00:43:35.281
I think more

00:43:35.281 --> 00:43:40.160
minorities will have access to

00:43:40.160 --> 00:43:45.080
capital so that they could build their
own businesses because I'm successful.

00:43:47.140 --> 00:43:52.040
I don't think that we're going to
change financial institutions,

00:43:52.040 --> 00:43:57.580
in terms of minorities owning
a financial institution,

00:43:57.580 --> 00:44:02.300
but I think what I can
do is open a door for

00:44:02.300 --> 00:44:07.280
other minorities to have an opportunity
to become business people.

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:10.130
You see what I'm saying?

00:44:10.130 --> 00:44:11.730
Right now, we're seeing that everywhere.

00:44:11.730 --> 00:44:15.210
If you look at the hip hop community,
who do they credit for

00:44:15.210 --> 00:44:18.830
changing their mindset
on becoming businessmen?

00:44:18.830 --> 00:44:24.580
They credit me and athletes do the same
thing because I opened the door,

00:44:24.580 --> 00:44:26.970
and also changed their mindset and

00:44:26.970 --> 00:44:31.850
attitude, toward just being an artist,
or just being an athlete.

00:44:31.850 --> 00:44:35.710
Now we know that we can go from
the basketball court to the boardroom.

00:44:35.710 --> 00:44:39.940
So I think EquiTrust will
allow others just to say hey,

00:44:39.940 --> 00:44:44.480
there's an opportunity to own
a financial institution, hopefully.

00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:49.700
If not just, again,
being a pioneer for minorities to

00:44:49.700 --> 00:44:55.350
be able to know that you can be successful
as a businessman or a business woman.

00:44:55.350 --> 00:45:00.320
I'm sitting in a great spot, in a great
position, I don't take it lightly.

00:45:03.060 --> 00:45:06.670
Just like being a minority in baseball,
it's great.

00:45:06.670 --> 00:45:08.830
We've changed the Dodgers.

00:45:08.830 --> 00:45:13.809
I don't know where we were, we weren't
in the top ten in attendance before.

00:45:14.860 --> 00:45:17.510
Now for
the three years that we've owned the team,

00:45:17.510 --> 00:45:23.585
we've been number one in attendance
in the MLB, over 42,000 a night.

00:45:23.585 --> 00:45:26.970
We've enhanced the fan experience.

00:45:26.970 --> 00:45:31.010
We've changed the team and
we won our division three years in a row.

00:45:31.010 --> 00:45:32.660
We've changed everything and

00:45:32.660 --> 00:45:39.020
now we're what, Forbes said we're
the sixth most valuable sports franchise

00:45:39.020 --> 00:45:44.660
in the world behind three soccer teams
and, of course, the Yankees and Cowboys.

00:45:44.660 --> 00:45:45.578
We'll take that all day.

00:45:45.578 --> 00:45:49.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The things that

00:45:49.100 --> 00:45:50.660
we've been able to do has been great.

00:45:51.860 --> 00:45:56.720
We'll see what happens in terms of
financial institutions but I'm just happy

00:45:56.720 --> 00:46:01.980
that I have a successful business and
that we're going to grow that business.

00:46:01.980 --> 00:46:08.350
We just sold another business that I
was into, which was Simply Health.

00:46:09.750 --> 00:46:13.660
We built that business over three years,
a Medicaid, Medicare business.

00:46:13.660 --> 00:46:18.230
My partner and I Mike Fernandez,
we took a business that we bought,

00:46:18.230 --> 00:46:19.540
it only had 17,000 members.

00:46:19.540 --> 00:46:22.520
Now about 300,000 members and we sold it.

00:46:22.520 --> 00:46:29.507
We grew it to 300,000 members and we just
sold it to Etna for about $1 billion.

00:46:31.000 --> 00:46:34.390
I've been doing a lot of great things and
this is what I do everyday.

00:46:35.730 --> 00:46:39.930
It's nice to not be in those hot pants
I used to wear and I wear suits now.

00:46:39.930 --> 00:46:41.750
Remember those short
shorts we used to wear?

00:46:41.750 --> 00:46:45.330
Johnny, you remember those shorts, man?

00:46:45.330 --> 00:46:49.980
I don't know how we got in those things.

00:46:49.980 --> 00:46:51.200
&gt;&gt; It's right here.

00:46:51.200 --> 00:46:52.660
&gt;&gt; That young man right there?

00:46:52.660 --> 00:46:53.480
Go ahead.

00:46:53.480 --> 00:46:54.630
&gt;&gt; My name is Aaron.

00:46:54.630 --> 00:47:00.200
I grew up in Inglewood down the street
from The Forum so this is special for me.

00:47:00.200 --> 00:47:01.250
Ciara, you are killing it.

00:47:01.250 --> 00:47:04.625
&gt;&gt; She is too, let us give her a hand.

00:47:04.625 --> 00:47:08.615
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; My question is can you talk about

00:47:08.615 --> 00:47:12.775
the conflict, or lack thereof, between
helping a community that you came from,

00:47:12.775 --> 00:47:16.165
that you grew up wit, a socioeconomically
disadvantaged community and

00:47:16.165 --> 00:47:18.335
also wanting to make money yourself?

00:47:18.335 --> 00:47:23.275
&gt;&gt; There's no conflict and
I'll tell you why there's no conflict.

00:47:23.275 --> 00:47:26.995
Minorities want me to be successful.

00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:33.220
When you think about retail options,
we were short retail options.

00:47:33.220 --> 00:47:36.925
Minorities, we were driving an hour,

00:47:36.925 --> 00:47:42.990
sometimes 45 minutes,
outside our community just to shop and

00:47:42.990 --> 00:47:47.050
go to a grocery store,
to go to the movies.

00:47:47.050 --> 00:47:51.106
&gt;&gt; What I was able to do was understand
what we wanted Because we were already

00:47:51.106 --> 00:47:54.108
driving to get it, but
it was outside our community.

00:47:54.108 --> 00:47:56.650
What I did was I just
brought it right to us.

00:47:58.560 --> 00:48:00.950
Minorities understand
that if I'm successful,

00:48:00.950 --> 00:48:06.035
then they have a chance to be successful.

00:48:06.035 --> 00:48:10.680
It'd be different if the prices were

00:48:12.580 --> 00:48:18.290
high or I'll price myself in
terms of to the urban consumer,

00:48:18.290 --> 00:48:19.750
but I hire people from the community.

00:48:19.750 --> 00:48:22.520
But first thing I did was to
train them to do their job.

00:48:24.740 --> 00:48:29.070
I'm going to say that one more time,
I trained them to do their job and

00:48:29.070 --> 00:48:31.140
then you get the retention
that you're looking for.

00:48:32.440 --> 00:48:38.620
And also I made it a workplace
that they have fun working at.

00:48:38.620 --> 00:48:43.630
And that again, gets you the retention
that you are looking for, as well.

00:48:43.630 --> 00:48:46.660
My Friday's was the hottest
place to work at and to go to.

00:48:46.660 --> 00:48:50.120
Right, my TGI Friday's that
I used to own right there.

00:48:50.120 --> 00:48:50.656
You been there?

00:48:50.656 --> 00:48:51.650
Right?

00:48:51.650 --> 00:48:54.249
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:48:54.249 --> 00:48:57.530
Man, a Laker game came on or Dodger game,

00:48:57.530 --> 00:49:01.090
you couldn't even get in the place,
packed.

00:49:01.090 --> 00:49:06.100
I was the first Fridays in the nation
to ever serve Dom Pérignon, Cristal,

00:49:06.100 --> 00:49:09.559
all the high end liquor,
I was the first one.

00:49:10.830 --> 00:49:11.900
They copied my motto.

00:49:13.640 --> 00:49:15.910
Because that's what my
customer base wanted.

00:49:15.910 --> 00:49:18.910
But the reason I'm saying it, is that now

00:49:21.080 --> 00:49:24.740
in urban America or Inglewood, or
wherever, you have a chance to now,

00:49:24.740 --> 00:49:29.950
in your own community, get a salad,
get dinner, go get Starbuck's coffee.

00:49:29.950 --> 00:49:31.530
And you didn't have the options before.

00:49:31.530 --> 00:49:38.290
So I look at it as, I'm putting
people to work and now that dollar,

00:49:38.290 --> 00:49:41.890
our problem in urban America,
the dollar only touches three hands.

00:49:41.890 --> 00:49:46.860
In suburban America,
one dollar touches about 20 hands.

00:49:46.860 --> 00:49:47.830
That's why I savor the grow.

00:49:49.070 --> 00:49:51.900
So I tried to make sure a dollar
could touch at least ten

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:54.290
hands in urban America by
putting a business there.

00:49:54.290 --> 00:49:55.680
Hiring people from the community.

00:49:55.680 --> 00:49:57.270
Now they're spending their money where?

00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:58.720
In the community.

00:49:58.720 --> 00:50:01.060
Now the community grows.

00:50:01.060 --> 00:50:05.670
And so
I'm happy about what I've been doing.

00:50:05.670 --> 00:50:12.360
I was able to put people
that have good paying jobs.

00:50:14.430 --> 00:50:21.060
Those district managers, or
the managers, they make good money.

00:50:21.060 --> 00:50:24.875
And then a brother like Ryan,
getting ready to be a millionaire, so,

00:50:24.875 --> 00:50:26.050
it's going to be cool.

00:50:26.050 --> 00:50:28.348
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I told him on the way here, man,

00:50:28.348 --> 00:50:31.156
if that deal work,
you going to be a millionaire.

00:50:31.156 --> 00:50:31.989
He said, okay.

00:50:31.989 --> 00:50:34.295
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's the first time he ever took

00:50:34.295 --> 00:50:36.430
the glasses off and was like, aw man.

00:50:36.430 --> 00:50:38.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He started sweating, Stanford,

00:50:38.840 --> 00:50:39.760
he started sweating.

00:50:40.960 --> 00:50:45.410
But that's also my job, is to make as
many minorities millionaires as I can.

00:50:45.410 --> 00:50:49.380
And so, we're on the right track already.

00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:54.360
I've already been able to do that
with a lot of people so far.

00:50:54.360 --> 00:50:56.390
One of the people I hired went to UCLA.

00:50:56.390 --> 00:50:58.120
Don't be booing.

00:50:58.120 --> 00:51:06.210
She went to UCLA business school,
19 years ago, she became my receptionist.

00:51:06.210 --> 00:51:09.680
I told her, if you stick with me,
it was just her and I at the time,

00:51:09.680 --> 00:51:12.800
that we were going to do
great things together.

00:51:13.850 --> 00:51:17.330
I said, don't worry about this was not
the job you wanted out of college.

00:51:18.890 --> 00:51:20.260
So guess what?

00:51:20.260 --> 00:51:22.030
Now she's a millionaire.

00:51:22.030 --> 00:51:24.750
Now she's my COO running my company.

00:51:26.060 --> 00:51:31.260
Went from the receptionist out front,
now she's making the decisions.

00:51:31.260 --> 00:51:33.290
So that's what can happen.

00:51:33.290 --> 00:51:37.020
And a lot of you don't get caught
up in what job you get coming out,

00:51:37.020 --> 00:51:38.790
you know you smart.

00:51:38.790 --> 00:51:45.865
Sometimes it's good just to get in, get
the knowledge, and then grow from there.

00:51:45.865 --> 00:51:48.545
[CROSSTALK] I gotta have
her because she's short.

00:51:48.545 --> 00:51:50.430
Come up on stage.

00:51:51.720 --> 00:51:52.601
Come on stage, come on.

00:51:52.601 --> 00:51:56.915
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, bring the phone.

00:51:56.915 --> 00:52:00.000
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, hand it to somebody.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:01.100
Don't put it in your pocket.

00:52:01.100 --> 00:52:01.869
&gt;&gt; Okay.
Here you go.

00:52:01.869 --> 00:52:03.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah, set it up.

00:52:03.170 --> 00:52:05.840
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; All right, next, come on, come on.

00:52:05.840 --> 00:52:12.689
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; All right, hold it, hold it.

00:52:12.689 --> 00:52:13.967
Turn around.
Back to back.

00:52:13.967 --> 00:52:19.144
[LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:52:19.144 --> 00:52:19.725
&gt;&gt; This is great.

00:52:19.725 --> 00:52:22.024
Well, I'm Maria.

00:52:22.024 --> 00:52:24.415
I'm a junior actually here at Stanford.

00:52:24.415 --> 00:52:24.949
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:52:24.949 --> 00:52:25.598
Put the mic up.

00:52:25.598 --> 00:52:29.747
&gt;&gt; And I kind of of just wanted to ask
you, you talk a lot about how powerful you

00:52:29.747 --> 00:52:34.170
are in the business community, but given
how strong of an influence you are to

00:52:34.170 --> 00:52:37.810
a lot of minorities and
just individuals all over the place.

00:52:37.810 --> 00:52:42.110
I kind of wanted to ask you what sort
of initiative you are kind of like,

00:52:42.110 --> 00:52:45.550
hoping to emerge yourself
in in respect to obesity,

00:52:45.550 --> 00:52:49.020
given that a lot of minorities
are facing this issue.

00:52:49.020 --> 00:52:51.400
And a lot of basketball players and
just athletes

00:52:52.420 --> 00:52:55.740
in general market a lot of their products
and they're not the healthiest, per se.

00:52:55.740 --> 00:52:57.740
It's a lot of sodas, and things like that.

00:52:57.740 --> 00:53:02.210
And I kind of wanted to ask you
since you have such a big impact and

00:53:02.210 --> 00:53:06.010
so much power like you were saying and
you want to empower those people,

00:53:06.010 --> 00:53:11.080
do you see yourself kind
of engaging in sort of any,

00:53:11.080 --> 00:53:16.680
I don't know, business opportunities,
whether it's like to like making those

00:53:16.680 --> 00:53:22.510
like culturally appropriate I guess foods,
or beverages, more healthy per se.

00:53:22.510 --> 00:53:24.930
&gt;&gt; Okay, well two things.

00:53:24.930 --> 00:53:28.030
First let's start with myself first.

00:53:28.030 --> 00:53:31.370
About two years ago,
I was 40 pounds heavier.

00:53:33.270 --> 00:53:35.350
And I knew that I was
snacking on the road.

00:53:35.350 --> 00:53:38.780
And I said to myself,
I've gotta make a change.

00:53:38.780 --> 00:53:39.660
So no, I'm serious.

00:53:39.660 --> 00:53:40.820
I gotta make a change.

00:53:40.820 --> 00:53:45.480
So I started putting nuts and fruit in
front of me, instead of those chips and

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:48.570
things, and I dropped about 40 pounds.

00:53:48.570 --> 00:53:51.410
Then I own a company called SodexoMAGIC.

00:53:52.440 --> 00:53:57.490
We provide food for colleges,
school systems, corporations,

00:53:57.490 --> 00:54:01.878
so we have I do Disneyland and
Disney World.

00:54:01.878 --> 00:54:02.710
Yay!

00:54:02.710 --> 00:54:05.375
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Mickey and Minnie eat my food.

00:54:05.375 --> 00:54:10.934
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And their headquarters, John Deere,

00:54:10.934 --> 00:54:16.490
All State Insurance,
Drexel University, on and on and on.

00:54:16.490 --> 00:54:22.490
So now we're starting to see now even
corporations, school systems, colleges,

00:54:22.490 --> 00:54:27.628
they want healthier options for
students, for their employees and so on.

00:54:27.628 --> 00:54:36.540
I think before I was not
mindful of what you said,

00:54:36.540 --> 00:54:42.180
but since I changed my diet myself and
now I get up every morning at 4:00.

00:54:42.180 --> 00:54:43.280
I'm in the gym by 5:00.

00:54:43.280 --> 00:54:47.270
I work cardio and
lift weights for an hour.

00:54:47.270 --> 00:54:51.760
So now I can talk about it
because it's the truth.

00:54:51.760 --> 00:54:52.650
I'm living it.

00:54:52.650 --> 00:54:55.670
Before I couldn't talk about it,
but I'm living it now.

00:54:55.670 --> 00:55:00.890
So it's very important
that we do eat healthier.

00:55:00.890 --> 00:55:05.470
Because African-Americans, we're dying
earlier than any other group of people.

00:55:07.690 --> 00:55:12.250
So we gotta make some changes
when you think about our diet.

00:55:12.250 --> 00:55:14.720
And also too, it has to start at home.

00:55:16.510 --> 00:55:18.650
And then that's when I was
talking to the young men about

00:55:20.200 --> 00:55:22.930
Harlem hadn't had a grocery store for
over 40 years.

00:55:24.320 --> 00:55:28.030
So I put Starbucks there,
I put my theater there in Harlem.

00:55:28.030 --> 00:55:34.090
That's when Pathmark finally
built a supermarket in Harlem.

00:55:34.090 --> 00:55:36.880
Now we got fresh fruit, vegetables.

00:55:36.880 --> 00:55:39.247
See that's the problem we
were having in urban America.

00:55:39.247 --> 00:55:44.839
We just didn't have the opportunities
to buy things that were fresh and

00:55:44.839 --> 00:55:49.777
healthy for us and now we're
hoping Whole Foods come into urban

00:55:49.777 --> 00:55:54.177
Communities and others,
Trader Joes, whoever it is.

00:55:54.177 --> 00:55:56.152
So, we can have those type of options.

00:55:56.152 --> 00:56:01.420
And so yes I'm promoting it and
I'm actually going to

00:56:01.420 --> 00:56:07.682
invest in a company that providing
that type of food option.

00:56:07.682 --> 00:56:10.485
I can't tell you much about it right now.

00:56:10.485 --> 00:56:11.943
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You know we're not there yet.

00:56:11.943 --> 00:56:13.740
Ryan, we're not there yet, right?

00:56:13.740 --> 00:56:19.122
Yeah, Ryan has, can I brag about him?

00:56:19.122 --> 00:56:20.885
Keep your eyes on him,
the guy with the glasses.

00:56:20.885 --> 00:56:25.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He has

00:56:25.130 --> 00:56:27.760
bought me lately about three deals.

00:56:27.760 --> 00:56:32.000
We just invested in
a company called Job Wealth,

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:37.450
that's a supplying minority talent to,
yes say that.

00:56:37.450 --> 00:56:40.335
&gt;&gt; Latino recruitment and
just minority recruitment in general.

00:56:40.335 --> 00:56:41.130
&gt;&gt; You heard about it.

00:56:41.130 --> 00:56:42.119
&gt;&gt; I heard about them.
I signed up.

00:56:42.119 --> 00:56:48.345
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Man, give me some.

00:56:48.345 --> 00:56:48.845
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I love it.

00:56:48.845 --> 00:56:49.582
No wonder we're

00:56:49.582 --> 00:56:50.391
friends.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:56:50.391 --> 00:56:51.391
&gt;&gt; We're friends,

00:56:51.391 --> 00:56:53.874
I follow you on Twitter actually.
&gt;&gt; No, but

00:56:53.874 --> 00:56:56.509
Ryan has done just a tremendous job.
And

00:56:56.509 --> 00:57:02.338
if we can do me one favor because when
it's your own and they become successful,

00:57:02.338 --> 00:57:07.997
you always got to feel proud because see,
guess what, you're the next one.

00:57:09.330 --> 00:57:14.746
And if you don't bless somebody else,
how can you be blessed, right?

00:57:14.746 --> 00:57:16.790
And that's what I'm all about.

00:57:16.790 --> 00:57:19.305
I'm blessed to have a great wife,

00:57:19.305 --> 00:57:24.002
I'm blessed to have great three kids,
two grandchildren now.

00:57:24.002 --> 00:57:29.081
But also, I'm so happy that I saw
the talent in this young man, and

00:57:29.081 --> 00:57:34.450
that you guys really shape and
mold it into, he's going to be awesome.

00:57:34.450 --> 00:57:35.726
So if you can do me a favor,

00:57:35.726 --> 00:57:38.516
there's two people you need
to give a standing ovation.

00:57:38.516 --> 00:57:40.749
This beautiful lady,
she's so smart and talented.

00:57:40.749 --> 00:57:42.943
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Come on, come on, come on.

00:57:42.943 --> 00:57:43.833
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:57:43.833 --> 00:57:45.516
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; She did a wonderful job.

00:57:45.516 --> 00:57:52.080
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Come on man.

00:57:52.080 --> 00:57:52.860
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:57:52.860 --> 00:57:54.700
&gt;&gt; And this one too, come on.

00:57:54.700 --> 00:57:55.683
&gt;&gt; Oh my god.

00:57:55.683 --> 00:58:01.773
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:58:04.791 --> 00:58:11.662
Listen, I came from a very poor family.

00:58:15.611 --> 00:58:21.740
Sometimes we had, The peanut butter but
we didn't have the jelly.

00:58:23.170 --> 00:58:27.561
Sometimes we had something to drink and
sometimes we didn't.

00:58:27.561 --> 00:58:31.185
Sometimes it was tough.

00:58:31.185 --> 00:58:34.116
Six sisters, three brothers.

00:58:34.116 --> 00:58:38.017
But the one thing I never
had was poor dreams.

00:58:38.017 --> 00:58:41.456
Even though I grew up poor,
I didn't have poor dreams.

00:58:41.456 --> 00:58:49.395
And one thing I never lacked
was confidence in myself.

00:58:49.395 --> 00:58:54.410
I always believed in me, always.

00:58:54.410 --> 00:58:59.850
And the one thing that I could control,
is my work ethic and my work habits.

00:59:02.700 --> 00:59:07.496
First one in the gym, last one to leave.

00:59:07.496 --> 00:59:09.720
I'm the first one at work,
last one to leave.

00:59:13.720 --> 00:59:14.680
It doesn't change.

00:59:14.680 --> 00:59:20.309
If you want to be successful,
You got to get there early,

00:59:20.309 --> 00:59:23.424
you got to outwork your competition.

00:59:23.424 --> 00:59:28.058
And then, get your husband, or
wife, boyfriend, girlfriend,

00:59:28.058 --> 00:59:30.806
to understand and buy into your dream.

00:59:30.806 --> 00:59:32.112
That's one thing Cookie did.

00:59:32.112 --> 00:59:36.925
I said baby tonight I know it's not a real
date but I gotta take you to the theater-

00:59:36.925 --> 00:59:39.305
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:59:39.305 --> 00:59:41.620
&gt;&gt; To the Magic Johnson theater when

00:59:41.620 --> 00:59:46.413
we get there you go to your seat I
gotta kiss the babies for two hours and

00:59:46.413 --> 00:59:48.409
hug everybody that came in.

00:59:48.409 --> 00:59:52.723
Then when we leave we got to go by
Starbuck's and sit in the parking lot and

00:59:52.723 --> 00:59:55.289
watch how many people come into the store.

00:59:55.289 --> 00:59:57.006
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:59:57.006 --> 01:00:02.600
&gt;&gt; That's going to be our date.

01:00:02.600 --> 01:00:04.603
But guess what?

01:00:04.603 --> 01:00:06.820
She was sitting right there with me.

01:00:08.820 --> 01:00:11.112
The hard work paid off.

01:00:11.112 --> 01:00:18.680
Five championships, led the Lakers
the nine finals in 12 years.

01:00:18.680 --> 01:00:20.685
What a run, right?

01:00:20.685 --> 01:00:24.310
Then here, the business world.

01:00:26.260 --> 01:00:29.200
Now I was struggling to get money and
capital.

01:00:29.200 --> 01:00:31.908
Now, I don't have to worry.

01:00:31.908 --> 01:00:36.532
If I need to raise a billion dollars
tomorrow, which we're starting a new fund,

01:00:36.532 --> 01:00:38.378
I wish I could tell you about it.

01:00:38.378 --> 01:00:40.780
But we already got 2 billion committed.

01:00:40.780 --> 01:00:44.142
We haven't even gone out,

01:00:44.142 --> 01:00:48.680
because I got a proven track record.

01:00:48.680 --> 01:00:49.893
&gt;&gt; Magic,
I'm going to interrupt there because-

01:00:49.893 --> 01:00:50.643
&gt;&gt; Okay, God bless you!

01:00:50.643 --> 01:00:54.556
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

01:00:54.556 --> 01:00:58.192
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; My track record won't compete.

01:00:58.192 --> 01:00:58.803
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

01:00:58.803 --> 01:01:02.710
[APPLAUSE]

01:01:02.710 --> 01:01:07.830
[MUSIC]

